# A minute you can ride — P10, in the kit's own pad notation.
#
# Ten legs, 3600 frames of a 60 Hz ride. `crates/openmx/tests/ride.rs` reads this file frame by frame,
# turns it into the DualShock 2 reports the shell's pad would have built, and rides it headless under
# `./kit check`. The same file is a script for a real machine:
#
#     ./kit pad serve &
#     ./kit pad script crates/openmx/tests/ride-minute.pad
#
# which is the same minute approximately — the kit's sender is wall-clock and not frame-exact, so what
# it proves is that the notation below is a script a human can also run, not a number.
#
# Cross is the throttle and Square the brake, which is this shell's own binding (`src/main.rs`), and
# the left stick is the handlebars. A `# leg:` line starts a leg; every other comment is a comment.

# leg: the launch
# Full throttle from a standstill, on the first frame: the shell's clutch latches if the throttle is
# ever shut while the bike is stopped, so a rider who reaches for it a moment later never moves at all
# (`it_pulls_away_after_a_frame_off_the_throttle` in the sibling test).
down cross
sleep 120f

# leg: flat out
# Twenty seconds of it, which is what this bike needs to stop accelerating.
sleep 1200f

# leg: turning right
# The stick hard over, the throttle still pinned: a rider holding a turn at speed.
stick l 1 0
sleep 240f

# leg: straight after the right
stick l 0 0
sleep 120f

# leg: turning left
stick l -1 0
sleep 240f

# leg: straight after the left
stick l 0 0
sleep 120f

# leg: the run-up
# The take-off ramp goes in here, 250 ft ahead of wherever the bike is and on whatever heading it has.
sleep 180f

# leg: the jump
# Over the lip, through the air, and down on its wheels.
sleep 300f

# leg: the brake
# The throttle shut and the brake pinned, from the top speed to a stop.
up cross
down square
sleep 600f

# leg: stopped
# Hands off the pad: it should still be standing there eight seconds later.
release
sleep 480f
